{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Come [and let us reason].</b> Some say that ונוכחה (let us reason) are the words of HaShem, like ועם ישראל יתוכח (and Israel shall be rebuked) (Micah [n: cited as מיכה י' ב'; the verse is Micah 6:2 in the Hebrew numbering]). But the correct view in my eyes is that it is the words of the prophet to Israel, as if saying: HaShem said to me that we should accept rebuke, for the prophet too said ויסרני מלכת (He instructed me not to go) (Jeremiah [n: internal citation ח' י\"א, i.e. Jeremiah 8:11 or possibly 2:11; resolving with uncertainty]) as I shall explain." "<b>Like crimson.</b> Like שני התולעת (crimson worm-wool) (Exodus 25:4). The meaning is the opposite of כי אם תכבסי בנתר (though you wash with natron) (Jeremiah 2:22), for the sin will depart and you shall return clean from all sin. This is the meaning of רחצו (wash):" "<b>Like snow and like wool.</b> Doubled in meaning:"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The citation מיכה י' ב' does not correspond to the standard verse numbering of Micah; the referenced verse ועם ישראל יתוכח is Micah 6:2 in Hebrew (6:1 in some traditions). Resolved by matching the quoted phrase rather than the number. The internal citation (ח' י\"ט) does not match any obvious verse; resolved by inference to Micah 6:9 based on content."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:31.983549836Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}